(
    schema: "xindeler_open_world.authored_procedural_layers.v1",
    // Which purely-procedural voxel layers still run inside Cromatolis.
    // `true` = the layer still runs. These compose with the global
    // assets/world/features.ron toggles, so a layer runs only when BOTH
    // allow it. Full rationale (and what each one costs) lives on
    // `AuthoredProceduralLayers` in world/src/sim/mod.rs -- not repeated
    // here, so the two can't drift.
    //
    // Cromatolis is hand-authored, and each of these passes stamps
    // hash-derived geometry into its block volumes without reading any
    // authored data -- so whether a given layer runs here is a content
    // decision made one layer at a time, not an all-or-nothing switch.
    // `caves`/`rocks` were off from COW-15 until 2026-09-19, when COW-23
    // flipped both after building the two guards that make them safe here
    // (the policy-aware authored-void guard in `world/src/layer/cave.rs`,
    // and boulder traversal accommodation in
    // `world/src/layer/rock_traversal.rs`). `caverns` stays off, for the
    // two independent reasons below. What each of the two flips bought,
    // and what it cost while it was off:
    //   - `caves: false` removed the region's entire *procedural*
    //     underground: cave biomes (most of the underground's visual
    //     identity), cave fauna (`apply_entity_spawns` in
    //     `world/src/layer/cave.rs`, which is biome-driven and entirely
    //     separate from `layer/wildlife.rs`), and cave loot (the
    //     structure-tied tables inside `write_column`). All three are back.
    //   - It does NOT cost the region its mineral wealth, and this comment
    //     used to claim it did ("`ChunkResource::Ore` goes to zero", "the
    //     authored replacement backfills none of it"). That has been false
    //     since COW-15/COW-20 (2026-09-13): the authored catalogue declares
    //     minerals for the large majority of its caves -- Iron, Coal,
    //     Cobalt, Silver, Gold and every gem included -- and they are placed
    //     as real sprites, nearly all of which map to
    //     `TerrainResource::Ore`/`::Gem`. The smithing ladder does not stop
    //     at Copper/Tin and the sim2 blacksmith inputs are not zero.
    //     (One exception worth knowing: `Velorite`/`VeloriteFrag` account to
    //     rtsim's `Loot` bucket rather than `Ore`/`Gem` -- still minable,
    //     still authored deliberately, just not part of the ore pools. See
    //     `world/src/layer/cromatolis_cave_features.rs`'s module doc.) The retraction is kept rather than
    //     just deleted because the old claim was load-bearing in COW-15's
    //     decision and people have read it.
    //     The live counts are whatever `cromatolis_v0_cave_features.ron`
    //     currently says -- deliberately NOT copied here, since that file is
    //     regenerated from the sibling repo's catalogue and a hand-copied
    //     tally would go stale silently. COW-23's spec section 8 has the
    //     2026-09-19 breakdown (657 declarations across 250 of 281 caves).
    //   - `caves: false` also meant the world map showed no cave markers at
    //     all here: the authored cave catalogue has no marker emitter yet,
    //     so the map's "show caves" filter was inert on this region.
    //     With `caves: true` it emits one marker per surface entrance the
    //     tunnel lattice derives. That count is pinned in exactly one place,
    //     `the_real_region_still_derives_its_surface_cave_markers` in
    //     `world/src/layer/cromatolis_cave_features.rs` -- not repeated here,
    //     so the two cannot drift. It reads **150** as of 2026-09-19; any
    //     doc still saying 149 predates that test and is wrong. COW-23's T20
    //     test (`world/src/lib.rs`) confirms the map publishes all of them
    //     end-to-end through `World::get_map_data`.
    // `spots: true` (flipped 2026-09-17, COW-16 -- see
    // `world/src/layer/spot.rs`'s `cromatolis_real_world` tests for the
    // measured before/after): `Spot::generate`'s early return on
    // `spots: false` meant Cromatolis had ZERO of the engine's localised
    // creature-cluster structures -- WolfBurrow, LionRock, DesertBones,
    // GnarlingTotem, SaurokAltar/Totem, MyrmidonTemple, WitchHouse, Igloo,
    // JungleTemple/Outpost and friends (`common::spot::Spot`, each with its
    // own `SpotConfig` entity list in `world/src/layer/spot.rs`) -- outside
    // settlements, on top of ambient wildlife already being sparse
    // (COW-16's other half, `cromatolis_wildlife_boost` /
    // `cromatolis_v0_density_boost.ron`). This is the actual root cause
    // behind the "1 gecko every 200m, no monsters anywhere" bug report:
    // GnarlingCarving courier-quest availability (the only consequence this
    // comment used to mention) was a minor side effect next to removing
    // every procedural monster camp region-wide. `is_valid`'s
    // `c.sites.is_empty()` check keeps every placed spot off any chunk a
    // registered Site (settlement/landmark/bridge/fortification) already
    // claims -- verified with zero collisions on the real map -- so
    // enabling this does not require its own exclusion guard. Measured on
    // the real map: 7630 spot markers across 681560 land chunks (~1.1%),
    // 7075 of them (~93%) carrying a non-empty entity list.
    // `caverns` is off for two INDEPENDENT reasons, so nobody has to
    // re-investigate this one (COW-23 spec section 3, whose >= 300-block
    // figure is wrong -- see below):
    //   1. features.ron has `caverns: false` globally, so this composes to
    //      off whatever it says here.
    //   2. Even if that global switch were flipped, caverns could not reach
    //      the authored volumes. In `apply_caverns_to`
    //      (`world/src/layer/mod.rs`) a cavern's *reference* altitude is
    //      `min(sea_level, alt/4) - height_range.end - surface_clearance`,
    //      but its CEILING is that plus `cavern_height`, which is bounded by
    //      `height_range.end` -- so the highest a cavern can reach is
    //      `min(sea_level, alt/4) - surface_clearance`. Against a deepest
    //      authored floor of `alt - SURFACE_MARGIN - GIANT_DEPTH`, the
    //      clearance works out to at least `0.75 * alt + 5` blocks: ~110 at
    //      sea level, more above it, and never zero for alt >= 0.
    //      NOTE: that is ~110, NOT the ">= 300" this comment and the spec
    //      both used to claim -- 300 is the separation to the reference
    //      altitude, which is not where the cavern's ceiling is. The
    //      conclusion (no collision, ever) survives; the margin is a third of
    //      what was advertised, so do not spend it.
    // In other words this line is not the thing keeping caverns out of the
    // authored volumes, and flipping it is not the risk it looks like.
    caverns: false,
    // Flipping any of these is a one-line change with no recompile; the
    // engine keeps the world-map cave markers and the tree/shrub
    // tunnel-avoidance consistent with whichever way these point.
    //
    // `caves: true` (flipped 2026-09-19, COW-23 T15). Safe because
    // `tunnel_bounds_at_from` consults the `AuthoredVoids` index before it
    // carves: a procedural tunnel is clipped out of any authored volume
    // whose entry in `cromatolis_v0_cave_features.ron` says
    // `procedural_contact: Seal`, and left alone where it says `Connect`.
    // The split between the two is deliberately NOT written here, for the
    // same reason the mineral tally above is not: it is regenerated from
    // the sibling repo's catalogue. `AuthoredVoids::policy_counts()` has
    // the live figure, and note it counts *indexed shapes*, which is a
    // different population from catalogue entries.
    // The authored carve also still runs *after* `apply_caves_to` and
    // rewrites its own footprint unconditionally, so no authored void can
    // be taken away by pass ordering either. That ordering is measured
    // end-to-end on real generated chunks by COW-23's T20 test in
    // `world/src/cromatolis_generation_tests.rs`; the guard itself is
    // measured separately (sealed caves breached: 6 before, 0 after) by the
    // real-asset tests in `world/src/layer/cromatolis_cave_features.rs`.
    // The two are different claims and neither substitutes for the other.
    //
    // NOTE for deploys: this changes generated terrain on a region that is
    // already live. `server/src/terrain_persistence.rs` re-applies stored
    // player block edits over regenerated terrain, so existing underground
    // builds or mined areas can now sit next to fresh tunnels and boulders.
    caves: true,
    // `rocks: true` (flipped 2026-09-19, COW-23 T15). Boulders land in
    // carved voids here -- deliberately: a rock wedged in a cave wall is
    // wanted. What is not wanted is one that seals the way through, and
    // `rock_traversal_repair` below is what tells those two apart. Unlike
    // `caves`, `apply_rocks_to` runs AFTER the authored carve, so a boulder
    // can legitimately occupy authored void; the guarantee is a walkable
    // bypass, not an untouched volume.
    rocks: true,
    spots: true,
    // On, and no longer inert now that `rocks` is on above: a rock wedged in
    // a cave ceiling or wall is wanted here, a rock that seals the way
    // through is not, and this is what tells them apart. Why boulders reach
    // carved voids at all, and why this one is opt-in where the four above
    // are opt-out, are both on the field in world/src/sim/mod.rs.
    rock_traversal_repair: true,
)
